## Research summary

I reproduced the failure locally with the actual test scaffold pointed at 1.12.2:

```
env GVM_TCK_LV=1.12.2 ./gradlew clean javaTest -Pcoordinates=org.apache.avro:avro:1.12.1
```

4 tests fail, all with the same new exception type:

```
java.lang.SecurityException: Forbidden org_apache_avro.avro.ReflectDataTest$TypeEncodedValue! This class is not trusted to be included in Avro schemas.
You may either use the system properties org.apache.avro.SERIALIZABLE_CLASSES and org.apache.avro.SERIALIZABLE_PACKAGES ...
	at org.apache.avro.util.ClassSecurityValidator$ClassSecurityPredicate.forbiddenClass
	at org.apache.avro.reflect.ReflectData.getClassProp
	at org.apache.avro.specific.SpecificData.lambda$getClass$2
```

This matches Avro 1.12.2's release notes: it hardened `SpecificData`/`ReflectData` class resolution from schema `java-class`/`java-element-class` properties behind a new `org.apache.avro.util.ClassSecurityValidator`, deny-by-default for anything outside a small built-in trusted set (`DEFAULT_TRUSTED_CLASSES`), configurable via the `org.apache.avro.SERIALIZABLE_CLASSES` / `org.apache.avro.SERIALIZABLE_PACKAGES` system properties or the `ClassSecurityValidator` API. The existing 1.12.1 tests use custom POJOs (`ReflectDataTest$TypeEncodedValue`, `SpecificDataTest$GeneratedRecordNoArg`) that used to resolve fine and are now rejected purely because they aren't on the trusted list — nothing to do with Native Image itself.

This is plain-JVM (`java run`) behavior, not a native-image trace, and the mechanism is an allowlist check, not runtime bytecode generation/class definition/agent-attach/etc. — fully repairable by generated test code trusting its own test package. No extra Maven dependency or Docker image is needed.

